* Fixed some missing punctuation in VWI that was annoying me. It's not related to the PR.
* Minor updates were made to DWAS, QWAS, MO1, MO2, and FI adjusting wording or reference paths.
* Changes were required to the meaning of MODE which are backwards-compatible.
* Changes were made to the meaning of CPUID1 which are **not** backwards-compatible.
  It seems we had planned for 16-bit paging to require DW, but this proposal is
  designed with this as an explicit **non**-goal.
  * MO2 moved from CP1.13 to CP1.12.
* Treatment of context identifiers was [discussed on Discord](https://discord.com/channels/828292123936948244/946806826756882552/1427394734061781102).
  Treatments other than the one applied in this PR are possible.
* Global pages and CIDs are primarily described in the [MODE register document](extensions/mode-control-register.md)
  which is linked from their feature documents. Perhaps this is abstraction inversion.
* 16-bit paging trades maximum addressable physical space for a lack of execute controls, global paging,
  and a severely restricted number of available context identifiers.
  This tradeoff is easy to shift if desired.
* There are 6 bits in the 16-bit `TLBHI` CR that I'm not sure what to do with.
  I considered making it a `G` bit for 16-bit soft paging but this would be rather
  bizarre as global pages aren't available to 16-bit hard paging and a soft paging
  implementation is already free to manually pin TLB entries.
  I think it might be best to leave these bits unused.
* Corresponding to above, 16-bit paging extension explicitly ignores the presence
  of Global Pages Enabled because it would allow a 16-bit soft TLB to invalidate
  entries that the software needs.
* 32-bit hard paging with global pages but without CIDs is almost identical to x86 paging without PAE.
* 32-bit soft paging has a few unused bits in TLBLO
* No encoding has been chosen yet for `TLBI`. We discussed a minor EXOP rework which
  `TLBI` would take advantage of, so I held off.